<v Woman 2>当然。是的，你经常听说。在杂志上或者报纸里。这些报道从来不会大肆宣扬。因为每个人都会想，不过是个舞女。哦，她肯定是个荡妇之类的。她们也依然是人，无论她们做什么。但没人应该被杀。

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:55.900
<v Sandy Tolan>Here, at the Marleybone, a dancer speaks in sharp, low tones, warning us not to ask too many questions about what happened to Susan.
<v Sandy Tolan>在这里，在马利波恩夜店，一名舞女尖锐而低沉地警告我们，不要再问关于苏珊发生了什么事的太多问题。

00:12:55.900 --> 00:13:04.340
<v Sandy Tolan>Here at Club After Dark, dozens of Russian women, part of the new wave of immigrant labor grind themselves into the laps of the men at the bar.
<v Sandy Tolan>在“天黑后”俱乐部，几十名俄罗斯女性，作为新一波移民劳工的一部分，在吧台男人的大腿上磨蹭。

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:16.710
<v Sandy Tolan>Here at Club 516, smoke and Spandex and dead eyes. A woman mechanically pulling her skirt up and down as she stares at herself in the mirror. I'm reminded of a photograph of Susan in the book Red Light.
<v Sandy Tolan>在516号俱乐部，烟雾、氨纶和死寂的眼神。一个女人机械地拉起又放下裙子，盯着镜子里的自己。我想起了《红灯》书中苏珊的一张照片。

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:37.300
<v Sandy Tolan>She's on a carpeted stage, guys with beards are perched on bar stools enjoying a laugh. And Susan, in a bikini, blonde hair falling over her shoulders, swivels her backside toward the men. She's poised before the mirror, dazed, alone, in another world.
<v Sandy Tolan>她站在铺着地毯的舞台上，留着胡子的男人们坐在吧台凳上开怀大笑。而苏珊穿着比基尼，金发垂在肩上，将后背朝向男人们扭动。她站在镜子前，茫然，孤独，仿佛身处另一个世界。

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:46.520
<v Sandy Tolan>Susan used to say that when she first got into go-go, it seemed like such a good idea. Her she is telling her story on a radio talk show two weeks before she disappeared.
<v Sandy Tolan>苏珊过去常说，她刚开始做舞女时，觉得这似乎是个好主意。这是她失踪前两周在一个广播脱口秀节目中讲述自己的故事。

00:13:46.520 --> 00:14:25.260
<v Susan Walsh>I had been working for six years as a writer for engineering and business publications after college and raising a son pretty much on my own. I was separated and I'd lost my job because of the recession. And I said, wow, how can I raise my son, go to graduate school, and take my writing out of the trade sector into the public sector and still do it all? Not just have time to make dinner and go to sleep every night. And I thought it was an easy way out because I had a friend who was making thousands a week. She said to me, why don't you dance a couple nights a week, and then you can do whatever you want. Raise your kid, go to school. I said, great.
<v Susan Walsh>大学毕业后，我做了六年的工程和商业出版物作家，几乎是一个人抚养儿子。我分居了，因为经济衰退丢了工作。我说，哇，我怎么才能抚养儿子、读研究生，并把我的写作从贸易领域带到公共领域，同时还能兼顾这一切？不仅仅是有时间做晚饭和每晚睡觉。我觉得这是一条捷径，因为我有位朋友每周赚几千美元。她对我说，你为什么不每周跳两晚舞，然后你想做什么就做什么。抚养孩子，去上学。我说，太棒了。

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:44.950
<v Susan Walsh>At first I felt totally in control. Lots of attention, men loved me. I'm getting attention. I'm the little princess on stage. It's insidious and after a while I realized that it was exhausting. There were many hidden costs and I stayed in it for far too many years and I'm not happy with the fact that I am still dancing.
<v Susan Walsh>起初我感觉完全在掌控之中。有很多关注，男人们爱我。我得到了关注。我是舞台上的小公主。这很阴险，一段时间后我意识到这让人精疲力竭。有很多隐形成本，我深陷其中太多年了，我不高兴自己还在跳舞。

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:54.950
<v Susan Walsh>Yes, I did complete half of my master's and I have been doing a lot of writing. But boy, what a high price I had to pay.
<v Susan Walsh>是的，我确实完成了硕士学位的二分之一，也做了很多写作工作。但天哪，我付出了多么高昂的代价。

00:14:54.950 --> 00:15:11.250
<v Sandy Tolan>At lunchtime, on the last day of July, I drive out to see Susan's mom, Martha Young, in Wayne, New Jersey. Susan's son, David, is visiting that day. Susan's been missing for two weeks now. We eat lunch and make small talk, then they invite me to join them at a video arcade at the mall.
<v Sandy Tolan>七月最后一天午餐时间，我开车去新泽西州韦恩市见苏珊的母亲玛莎·扬。苏珊的儿子大卫那天也在。苏珊已经失踪两周了。我们吃过午饭寒暄了几句，他们邀请我一起去商场的电子游戏厅。

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:15.050
<v Martha>What do you want to do [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. Should we get the tickets first?
<v Martha>你想做什么？我们要先去买票吗？

